.TH FLOOR 3M
.SH NAME
fabs, floor, ceil, trunc, round, copysign, fmin, fmax, fmod, fma \- absolute
value, rounding, remainder and fused multiply-add
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.B double fabs(double x);
.PP
.B double floor(double x);
.PP
.B double ceil(double x);
.PP
.B double trunc(double x);
.PP
.B double round(double x);
.PP
.B double copysign(double x, double y);
.PP
.B double fmin(double x, double y);
.PP
.B double fmax(double x, double y);
.PP
.B double fmod(double x, double y);
.PP
.B double fma(double x, double y, double z);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fabs
returns the absolute value
.RI | \|x\| |.
.PP
.I Floor
returns the
largest integer
not greater than
.IR x .
.PP
.I Ceil
returns the
smallest integer
not less than
.IR x .
.PP
.B Note:
everything from here down is C11 and had no v7 ancestor; v7's libm stopped at
.I floor
and
.IR ceil .
.PP
.I Trunc
returns
.I x
rounded toward zero.
.PP
.I Round
returns
.I x
rounded to the nearest integer, halfway cases away from zero.
.PP
.I Copysign
returns
.I x
with the sign of
.IR y .
.PP
.I Fmin
and
.I fmax
return the lesser and the greater of their two arguments.
.PP
.I Fmod
returns the remainder of
.IR x / y ,
with the sign of
.IR x .
.PP
.I Fma
returns
.IR x \(** y \(pl z ,
with the product computed exactly and only the sum rounded.
.SH SEE ALSO
abs(3), frexp(3), exp(3), intro(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Fmod
returns 0 and sets
.I errno
to EDOM when
.I y
is zero.  Nothing else on this page has an error return; none of them can
overflow, since each result is bounded by an argument.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS The sign is not a bit
The mantissa is 40-bit two's complement, so a number's sign is not a separable
field \- negating rewrites the whole mantissa \- and there is no negative zero.
.PP
.I Fabs
therefore needs no mask, and has no comparison to get wrong.
.PP
.I Copysign
is the comparison
.BR "y < 0" ,
and cannot be the usual bit graft.
.B Note:
that spelling would be
.I wrong
on an IEEE machine, where copysign(1, \-0.0) must be \-1; here the case does not
arise.
.SS There are no NaNs
C11 asks
.I fmin
and
.I fmax
to treat a NaN operand as missing data and return the other one.  This format has
no NaNs, so what is left of each routine is the comparison.
.SS floor, ceil and trunc stand on modf
All three are
.IR modf (3),
which lives in
.I libc
rather than here because the conversions need it.
.I Modf
already knows that any magnitude at or above 2**40 is integral in a 40-bit
mantissa, so a large argument comes back with a zero fraction and falls through
untouched.
.PP
.I Round
is written over
.I modf
too, and deliberately not as floor(x + 0.5), which is wrong twice: it rounds
halfway cases toward +infinity rather than away from zero, and for an
.I x
just below 0.5 the addition itself rounds up to 1.0 and the answer comes back 1
instead of 0.
.SS fmod was rewritten, not ported
v7's libm had no
.I fmod
at all.  The
.I fmod.c
this tree inherited was an fdlibm-derived import that takes an IEEE double apart
through a union of 32-bit halves \- and there is no 32-bit word to alias a double
onto here, no sign-magnitude sign bit, no biased-by-1023 exponent field, and no
subnormals to renormalize.  It is replaced by the classic scale-and-subtract loop
over
.IR frexp (3)
and
.IR ldexp (3),
which needs no bit access at all.
.PP
The loop is exact by Sterbenz's lemma: each step subtracts the largest
y*2**n not exceeding the running value, and such a subtraction loses no bit
whatever.  It terminates in at most 127 steps, one per exponent.  This is what
x \- y*trunc(x/y) cannot do: that form has already lost the low bits of the
quotient before the multiply, and for x/y much above 2**40 it has lost all of
them.
.SS fma is exact, and its split is not Dekker's
There is no wider type to compute in \-
.BR float ,
.B double
and
.B "long double"
are the same one 48-bit word.  What makes the routine possible anyway is that the
mantissa is 40 bits and 40 halves evenly: split each operand into two 20-bit
pieces and all four cross products are exactly representable, so x*y is recovered
as a sum of two machine numbers with nothing dropped.
.PP
.B Note:
the textbook Dekker split multiplies by 2**20 + 1 and relies on the multiply
rounding to nearest.  This machine rounds by forcing the low mantissa bit when
anything is discarded, which is faithful but not nearest, and the theorem does
not survive it.  The split used here assumes nothing about rounding:
.I ldexp
moves the binary point,
.I modf
cuts at it, and both are exact.
.PP
What is guaranteed is that the product is exact and its low half reaches the
addition instead of being rounded away before it.  The residual accumulation and
the closing sum are faithful rather than provably single-rounded, for the same
reason the split could not be Dekker's.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libm.a
.br
lib/libm/fabs.c, floor.c, trunc.c, round.c, copysign.c, fmin.c, fmax.c, fmod.c,
fma.c
